sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch15-09> delphi:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-ch15-09: partially-resolvable, resolvability 0.58" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.582"^^xsd:double ;
    delphi:hasVerdict "partially-resolvable" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.62"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "Drop profiles and a flattening index are standard geometric measurements, so the main series can be compared with an explicit physical family of shapes. Public images and scans cover only part of the regular echinoids, and plate-level mechanical properties are mostly in separate papers. A quantitative fit can still reject a pure drop model for the conical and collapsed forms, while agreement on the ordinary flattened series would remain compatible with several rounded geometries." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: Measure test height, diameter and plate thickness on vertical outlines of regular echinoids, fit each profile to a sessile-drop solution over a range of Bond numbers, and treat plate thickness as a proxy for shell tension. The claim would be supported if flattening increases as plate rigidity falls and the residuals match drop profiles; it would be weakened if spherical and flattened tests share the same plate thickness or if the outlines fit ellipses or cones as well as drops." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "Open scans and profile photographs are sparse for flexible forms such as Phormosoma and for Palaeechinus.", "Aristotle's lantern and apical buoyancy, which Thompson himself invoked for some outlines, are extra forces a single drop parameter does not absorb." ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok--dataset-1>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok--dataset-2> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok--operationalizability> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Operationalizability of thompson1917-ch15-09 by Grok 4.7: 0.72" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0-operationalizability> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0/operationalizability" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.72"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:isPartOf <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch15-09--grok> ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> .
